      "problem": "Use mathematical induction to prove that ${n}^{4}+7n$ is even for all $n\\in N.$\n\nLet $P(n)$ denote the following sequence of statements:\n\n$$\nP(n):\\,2\\,∣\\,{n}^{4}+7n\n$$\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=\\,${{field-1}}.\n\nSubstituting $n=\\,${{field-2}} into the expression ${n}^{4}+7n,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-3}}={{field-4}},\\,\\text{and}\\,{{field-5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is {{field-6}} for $n=\\,${{field-7}}.\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-8}}, we have that\n\n$$\nP(k):\\,{{field-9}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-10}} & ={k}^{4}+7k+2({{field-11}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since {{field-12}} by the inductive hypothesis, and {{field-13}} we have that {{field-14}}, as required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${n}^{4}+7n$ is even for all {{field-15}}.",
      "worked_solution": "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1),P(2),P(3),…,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$P(n):\\,2\\,∣\\,{n}^{4}+7n$ for all $n\\in N$\n\nWe start by proving the base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into the expression and show it is divisible by $2:$\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$ Substituting $n=1$ into the expression ${n}^{4}+7n,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(1):\\,(1)^{4}+7(1)=8,\\,\\text{and}\\,2\\,∣\\,8\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have that\n\n$$\nP(k):\\,2\\,∣\\,{k}^{4}+7k.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\nP(k+1):\\,2\\,∣\\,(k+1)^{4}+7(k+1).\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the expression $(k+1)^{4}+7(k+1)$ as ${k}^{4}+7k$ (from the inductive hypothesis) plus a multiple of $2.$ We do this as follows:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k+1)^{4}+7(k+1) & ={k}^{4}+4{k}^{3}+6{k}^{2}+4k+1+7(k+1) \\\\  & =({k}^{4}+7k)+4{k}^{3}+6{k}^{2}+4k+8 \\\\  & =({k}^{4}+7k)+2(2{k}^{3}+3{k}^{2}+2k+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $2\\,∣\\,({k}^{4}+7k)$ by the inductive hypothesis, and $2(2{k}^{3}+3{k}^{2}+2k+4)$ is clearly divisible by $2,$ their sum is also divisible by $2,$ as required.\n\nSince we have shown that the expression in $(∗)$ is divisible by $2,$ our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${n}^{4}+7n$ is even for all $n\\in N.$",
